                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Lethal RPG: War is a free turn based RPG that feels like it came from the era of browser games but has enough content to be much more than a quick little adventure. I take control of Lethal and a party of warriors as I travel through a fantasy world, complete quests and fight different creatures. The presentation is fairly simple, but there is a surprisingly large amount packed into the game. I have 13 areas to explore and more than 50 story quests, so there is a proper adventure here rather than just a small collection of battles.\n\nThe combat is where most of my time goes, and I like that I am controlling a full party rather than just one character. There are eight playable characters, each with their own abilities, weapons and armour, so I can build my group around different roles. The battles are turn based and give me time to think about what I want each character to do before the turn plays out. It is not an incredibly complicated system, but there are enough different abilities and enemies to make the fights more interesting than simply attacking until something dies.\n\nI also like the amount of progression around the combat. I can find materials from boss encounters and use them to craft stronger weapons and armour, while pets can give my party extra abilities during battles. The arena is another nice addition because it lets me take on harder versions of enemies I have already fought. I enjoy having these extra systems because they give me reasons to keep improving my characters instead of just following the main quests. There are also nine achievements, which are simple but give me a few extra targets to work towards.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is the age of the game showing through. The visuals are very basic and the overall presentation does not have much personality compared with newer RPGs. The controls and interface also feel like they were designed with a browser game in mind, which makes some parts feel a little clunky. I can get used to it fairly quickly, but there are moments where I wish the menus and general presentation were smoother. The combat itself can also become repetitive once I have worked out which abilities are most useful against the enemies I am facing.\n\nThe story is another area where I think the game is more functional than memorable. There is a large amount of content and plenty of quests, but I am mostly playing for the progression and battles rather than because I am completely invested in the writing. That is not necessarily a huge issue for me because the game is clearly focused on its RPG systems. I would rather have a simple story with lots of things to do than a complicated story attached to a very short game, and Lethal RPG: War definitely gives me plenty of content to work through.\n\nWhat makes the package easier to appreciate is that it is completely free. I am getting the full single player game without having to pay for access, and the amount of content is honestly pretty good for that. It has the 13 areas, over 50 story quests, eight characters, crafting, pets and the arena, all built around a straightforward turn based system. It is not polished enough for me to put it alongside my favourite RPGs, but I think the amount of actual game here deserves credit.\n\nOverall, Lethal RPG: War is a simple but surprisingly substantial RPG that I think works best when I judge it for what it is. The turn based combat, party system, crafting, pets and large number of quests give me plenty to work through, while the free price makes the rougher presentation much easier to overlook. The biggest things holding it back are the dated visuals, basic story and combat that can become repetitive, but I still think there is a genuinely enjoyable adventure underneath those limitations. For me, it is a good example of a small RPG that offers much more content than its presentation initially suggests.\n\nRating: 7.7/10",
